.TH elfshaker-extract 1  "elfshaker 1.0.0-rc1" "elfshaker Manual"
.SH NAME
elfshaker\-extract \- Restore a snapshot into the working directory.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBelfshaker extract\fR [\fB\-\-reset\fR] [\fB\-\-verify\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-T\fR|\fB\-\-threads\fR] [\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISNAPSHOT\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Restores a snapshot into the working directory and updates HEAD. A snapshot may be named by its tag or as PACK:SNAPSHOT. If the snapshot is not available locally, elfshaker refreshes configured remotes and retries.
.PP
By default, extraction transitions from HEAD incrementally: files removed from the new snapshot are deleted, and changed files are checked for unexpected local modifications. Existing untracked destination paths cause extraction to fail before files are changed; use \-\-force to authorize replacement.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-reset\fR
Write every file in the snapshot, retaining untracked\-path protection. Files outside the snapshot are not removed.
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\fR
Verify each extracted file against its stored SHA\-1 checksum.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite despite checks for unexpected working\-directory modifications.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 0]
Use N decompression workers; 0 selects the number of physical CPU cores.
.TP
\fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show informational progress and diagnostic messages.
.TP
\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: elfshaker_data]
Use PATH as the data directory containing packs, indexes, loose objects, remotes, and HEAD. This does not change the working directory.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISNAPSHOT\fR>
Snapshot tag or PACK:SNAPSHOT identifier to restore.
